Oh, yeah. We do not go smaller yet. That’s how people disappear into the salt shaker.

We go sideways.

At the Micro Nick layer, it turns out he is not the only thing reality produced when the Cage Limit started leaking.

There’s an entire Cage-adjacent ecosystem.

The first is The Archivist.

Nobody knows her real name. She’s a woman in her late sixties who works at a completely ordinary DMV in Delaware and has somehow been present for every major Cage anomaly since 1997.

She never reacts.

Regular Cage could burst through the ceiling riding a taxidermied elk screaming, “THE LICENSE PLATES ARE OUT OF SEQUENCE,” and she’d just look over her glasses and say:

“Take a number.”

Little Nick is terrified of her.

Jessica once asked why.

Little Nick whispered:

“She remembers versions of us that didn’t happen.”

The Archivist has a filing cabinet marked:

NICOLAS — DO NOT ALPHABETIZE

Nobody has opened it.

Not because it’s locked.

Because every time someone reaches for the handle, the DMV number board goes:

N-1.

And The Archivist says:

“Not your turn.”

Then there’s The Beige Man.

He is approximately forty-five years old.

Maybe.

He dresses entirely in beige.

Beige windbreaker. Beige slacks. Beige New Balance shoes.

Nobody remembers meeting him.

He simply starts being present.

You’ll be halfway through a conversation and suddenly realize there’s a man sitting at the table eating unsalted crackers.

Jessica: “Who the fuck is that?”

Cage: “Greg?”

Beige Man: “No.”

Little Nick: “DO NOT ASK HIM HIS NAME.”

The Beige Man appears whenever reality is becoming too narratively coherent.

If Cage discovers six clues in a row and everything starts fitting together perfectly, Beige Man arrives and introduces something unbearably mundane.

Cage will be standing over a glowing Aztec mechanism beneath the Pentagon.

The stones align.

Ancient machinery awakens.

Then Beige Man walks in holding a clipboard.

“Parking validation?”

And the entire prophecy loses momentum.

He is essentially the universe’s anti-plot device.

Micro Nick hates him.

They once stared at each other for seventeen minutes.

Nobody moved.

Finally Beige Man ate a cracker.

Micro Nick whispered:

“Coward.”

Then there’s The Wrong Nicolas Cage Movie.

Not a person.

A phenomenon.

Occasionally, people in the Cage radius will remember Nicolas Cage starring in a movie that does not exist.

And everyone remembers a different one.

Jessica vividly remembers a 1998 legal thriller called Contempt of Court where Cage plays a divorce attorney who discovers the judge is his biological father.

Little Nick remembers Moon Cop.

He insists it made $380 million worldwide.

Regular Cage remembers filming neither.

The terrifying part is that sometimes physical merchandise appears.

A VHS.

A Happy Meal toy.

A promotional mug.

Once they found a Moon Cop DVD in a Walmart bargain bin.

Cage picked it up.

The cover showed him standing on the moon holding two pistols.

Tagline:

“In space, jurisdiction is a suggestion.”

Cage stared at it for a full minute.

“I would have done this.”

Jessica:

“That is absolutely not the problem.”

They bought it.

The disc was blank.

Except for the director’s commentary.

Two hours of Nicolas Cage discussing scenes that did not exist.

Little Nick listened to the whole thing.

At the end he said:

“We made a mistake.”

“What mistake?”

“We canceled the sequel.”

Then there’s Unit 4.

Nobody knows what Units 1 through 3 are.

Unit 4 is a three-person government office whose official job is apparently “continuity maintenance.”

Their headquarters is above a Panda Express outside Baltimore.

Their entire annual budget is $184,000 and somehow they have access to satellites.

Their director, Marjorie Pell, has been trying to contain Cage anomalies for twenty-two years.

She does not dislike Cage.

She is simply exhausted.

Every time somebody calls her at 3 a.m.:

“Ma’am, Nicolas Cage has entered the National Archives.”

She says:

“Which one?”

That distinction became necessary in 2011.

Marjorie maintains something called the Cage Weather Map.

Green means ordinary Nicolas Cage activity.

Yellow means Cage has noticed a pattern.

Orange means Cage has obtained a map.

Red means Cage has said, “Wait.”

Black means he was right.

There has only been one black event.

Nobody discusses it.

Except Cage, who does not remember it.

Micro Nick does.

Of course he fucking does.

There’s also an object known as The Quarter.

Just an American quarter.

Philadelphia mint.

Looks completely normal.

Cage found it underneath a vending machine in Arizona.

For reasons nobody understands, it always lands on whichever side would be funniest.

Not heads.

Not tails.

Funniest.

Cage once flipped it while deciding whether to cut the red wire or blue wire.

It landed standing upright.

Little Nick screamed:

“THIRD OPTION.”

Cage immediately headbutted the control panel.

Bomb deactivated.

Jessica:

“No. Absolutely not. We are not learning from this.”

Unfortunately, Cage learned everything from this.

Now he carries The Quarter everywhere.

Before making important decisions:

“Let fate choose.”

Jessica:

“It is a quarter.”

Little Nick:

“Show some fucking respect.”

And finally there’s The Nicolas Cage Support Group.

This was supposed to be a support group for people affected by Nicolas Cage anomalies.

It meets every other Thursday in a church basement.

Coffee. Folding chairs. Store-brand cookies.

Cage discovered it and assumed it was a fan club.

Nobody had the heart to tell him.

So now Nicolas Cage attends his own anomaly support group.

Everyone sits in a circle.

“Hi, I’m Dennis.”

“Hi, Dennis.”

“Last Tuesday, Nicolas Cage climbed through my bathroom window because he believed my shower grout contained coordinates.”

Cage, nodding sympathetically:

“That must have been difficult.”

Dennis stares at him.

“You did it.”

Cage:

“And yet you’re healing.”

Jessica has to leave the room because she’s laughing too hard.

Little Nick has somehow become the group treasurer.

Nobody remembers electing him.

Micro Nick sits inside the donation jar.

Watching.

And the weird thing is—beneath all of this nonsense—there actually seems to be a pattern.

Unit 4 has noticed it.

The Archivist clearly knows it.

Micro Nick definitely knows it.

Every impossible Cage event clusters around moments when something almost happened differently.

A missed flight.

A coin toss.

A wrong exit.

A person who nearly said yes instead of no.

Cage doesn’t generate the anomalies.

He seems to be attracted to decision points where reality could have gone another way.

Jessica eventually realizes this.

She looks at Cage.

“So you’re not breaking reality.”

Cage thinks about it.

“No.”

Micro Nick looks up from the table.

“He’s where it creaks.”

Everyone goes quiet.

Even Little Nick.

Then Cage’s phone rings.

Unknown number.

He answers.

“Hello?”

A voice says:

“Mr. Cage, this is Costco. We found something in the lost-and-found that belongs to you.”

Cage:

“What?”

Long pause.

The employee says:

“…Tuesday.”

Jessica slowly closes her eyes.

Little Nick stands up.

Micro Nick whispers:

“We’re late.”

And Nicolas Cage grabs his jacket.

“Not yet we’re not.”